In article <2jaab2$84o@wswiop09.win.tue.nl>, dedos4@wswiop09.win.tue.nl (Boudewijn Wayers) writes:

>Recently, someone asked why it seemed that wraiths so seldom left a
>corpse. After having had a LONG look at the sources, I think I can
>conclude the following:
>
>First, some monsters NEVER leave a corpse (these monsters are mentioned
>in the WCST 7.00 spoilers). Next, some monsters ALWAYS leave a corpse,
>when killed the "usual" way: these are lizards, the three riders in
>the endgame, and "big" monsters (all monsters that are defined as
>Large, Huge or Gargantuan: have a look at the sources to know which
>are). Of course, if you turn anything to stone, you won't be able to
>eat its corpse, since there will be none. Also, on the Rogue level no
>corpses are ever left.
>
>Now some monsters may not be killed by you, but by another cause (things
>that spring to mind are applying some tool, making a wrong move (into a
>trap), gettting caught in a blast, being hit by another monster when
>you are causing conflict, or throwing something cursed at you and being
>hit themselves). If this is the case, there is a 1/3 chance that the

Not totally sure, but I think this is 2/3.  I'm looking at mon.c:
   991       if(rn2(3)
   992  #ifdef REINCARNATION
   993          && !Is_rogue_level(&u.uz)
   994  #endif
   995          && !(level.flags.graveyard && is_undead(mdef->data) && rn2(3)))
   996               (void) make_corpse(mdef);
   997  }

>monster will leave a corpse, except when you are on a graveyard level
>and the monster is an undead, in which case the chance is 2/9.
Right: 2/3*!(2/3)= 2/9
>
>Now suppose you killed the monster yourself. Again, the game first
>looks if you are on a "graveyard" level, and whether the monster you
>have killed is an undead. If this is the case, there is a 1/3 chance
>that no corpse will be left to start with.
>
>Now you must do some counting. Take the monster frequency (this can be
>looked up in the sources, in monst.c, and ranges from 0 to 7), add 1 if
>the monster size if Tiny (again, look in monst.c), and add 2 to finish
>with. There is a one-in-(this number) chance that a corpse will be
>generated.

Again, I'm not totally sure, but I read this slightly differently:
  1229             if (bigmonst(mdat) || mdat == &mons[PM_LIZARD]
  1230                        || is_golem(mdat)
  1231                        || is_mplayer(mdat)
  1232                        || is_rider(mdat))
  1233                     chance = 1;
  1234             else chance = !rn2((int)
  1235                     (2 + ((mdat->geno & G_FREQ)<2) + verysmall(mdat)));
  1236             if (chance)
  1237                     (void) make_corpse(mtmp);

Isn't that chance = 1/(2+ 1*(is G_FREQ<2) + 1*(is very small) )
In other words, for the wraith example 
           chance = 1/(2 + 1*(0) + 1*(0)) 
                  = 1/(2 + 0 + 0)
                  = 1/2
(or if there are graveyards on the level 1/2*2/3 = 2/6)

>
>
>An example (the wraith-frequency is 2, and a wraith isn't Tiny):
>
>Suppose a wraith is killed by a trap, with no graveyard on the level.
>  There is a 1/3 chance that a corpse will be left.
>
>Suppose a wraith is killed by you, with no graveyard on the level.
>  There is a 1/(2+0+2) = 1/4 chance that a corpse will be left.
>
>Suppose a wraith is killed by a trap, with a graveyard on the level.
>  There is a 2/3*1/3 = 2/9 chance that a corpse will be left.
>
>Suppose a wraith is killed by you, with a graveyard on the level.
>  There is a 2/3*1/4 = 1/6 chance that a corpse will be left.
>
>So the chances of getting a wraith corpse are (if my reading of the
>source is correct):
>
>Cause	Gravey	Chance
>Other	No	1/3 = 12/36
>You	No	1/4 =  9/36
>Other	Yes	2/9 =  8/36
>You	Yes	1/6 =  6/36
>
>From the above, it is obvious that you should let wraiths be killed in
>stead of killing them yourself, if you want the greatest chance of them
>leaving a corpse.
